;; any-fn-tag-does-not-discriminate-signatures: the undefined-behaviour half.
;;
;; `cast` is documented and implemented as a CHECKED downcast that panics on a
;; mismatch.  For a function payload it did not check at all -- every function
;; carried the bare TY_FN tag, so `__tur_any_cast_check(7, 7)` passed and the
;; int->int closure came back typed `(-> cstr cstr)`.  Calling it printed "i":
;; the pointer's low byte, read as a `const char *` the callee never received.
;;
;; It panics now, on BOTH back ends.  The messages differ and only the compiled
;; one is asserted (stderr.compiled-only: run-turi.sh asserts stdout and the
;; exit code here, not expected.stderr), because the two sides say the same thing
;; differently: compiled has only the two ids' display names, which are both
;; "fn", so it reports the mismatch as a signature mismatch; the interpreter has
;; the closure's reconstructed key and names both signatures outright.
;;
;; No requires.compiled: running this under --interpret is the point.  The
;; interpreter's fn target used to fall into `default: ok = true`, which passed
;; everything -- `(cast 7 (-> int int))` included.
(defn mk [] : any (fn [x : int] : int (+ x 1)))

(defn main [] : int
  (let [f (cast (mk) (-> cstr cstr))]
    (println (f "hi")))
  0)
